From: Stefan <104927860+Steefzar@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq/amd-pstate: don't write a read-only auto_sel that
 already has the wanted value

On shared-memory CPPC systems shmem_init_perf() reads auto_sel and then
always writes it, returning the write's error. Some firmware declares
auto_sel in _CPC as a constant integer rather than a register. Reading it
works, but cppc_set_reg_val() rejects any non-buffer register with
-EOPNOTSUPP, so driver init fails on every CPU in every mode:

  amd_pstate: failed to set auto_sel, ret: -95
  amd_pstate: Failed to initialize CPU 0: -95
  ...
  amd_pstate: failed to register with return -19

The system then falls back to acpi-cpufreq. Seen on the Surface Laptop 4
AMD (Ryzen 5 4680U, Renoir), whose _CPC hard-codes auto_sel on.

Skip the write when the value already matches the mode. Active and guided
mode want auto_sel on and can now initialise on such firmware. Passive
mode wants it off, so it still fails there, as it must.

Local to linux-cachyos-surface-latest; not from linux-surface. See STATUS.
---
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c
@@ -540,6 +540,15 @@
 		return 0;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Some firmware declares auto_sel in _CPC as a read-only integer, so
+	 * the write below fails with -EOPNOTSUPP even when the register already
+	 * holds the wanted value, and init then fails in every mode. Only write
+	 * when the value actually has to change.
+	 */
+	if (auto_sel == (cppc_state != AMD_PSTATE_PASSIVE))
+		return 0;
+
 	ret = cppc_set_auto_sel(cpudata->cpu,
 			(cppc_state == AMD_PSTATE_PASSIVE) ? 0 : 1);
 
